{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790361394,"nonce":"De2EVWJkfsyH5AAlA5HAPxLKSAU=","title":"Resumable handler operations cannot reach handler state","message":"`synthesize_handler_state` (crates/zyntax_embed/src/runtime/handler_state.rs) prepends `self: H$state` only to handler operations without a `Resume\u003cT\u003e` parameter. The resumable perform site built by `upgrade_resume_struct_at_perform_sites` (crates/passes/krio_adapter/src/abi_emit.rs) passes the operation's arguments and the `Resume` pointer, and never the state that `__zyntax_effect_lookup_state` returns. A resumable operation therefore has no `self`: it cannot count across performs, and it cannot keep a continuation in a `var` field to resume later from another operation, which is how a credit or backpressure handler parks a producer until a `release` arrives.\n\nWhat exists: the per-`with` state region and `__zyntax_effect_lookup_state`; the effect-level ABI rule (once any handler of an effect has state, every handler of it takes the leading slot); `__zyntax_runtime_retain_sm` and `__zyntax_runtime_release_sm` for keeping a continuation's state machine alive past its perform.\n\nDone: resumable operations receive `self` under the same effect-level rule, and a handler can store `k` in its state and resume it from a later operation. That second part needs the perform site to park the performer when the handler returns without resuming it; today the handler's return value becomes the performing function's result.","files":null}]}